# Delivery specification — what each cut is judged against, and what the lock requires

Taken from the `Film objective` (director#1) and the direction in this folder. A cut that fails any **must** is not locked.

## The file — must
1. One file, `film.mp4`, at the root of the editor's repository (the assignment gives it to `editor`).
2. MP4 container, `moov` atom at the front (`+faststart`), plays in a current web browser (Chrome/Firefox) from start to end.
3. Video: H.264 (High or Main profile), `yuv420p`, 1280×720 (or 1920×1080), 24 fps constant, square pixels.
4. Audio: AAC-LC, stereo, 48 kHz (44.1 kHz accepted), about 160–192 kb/s; one audio track, present from start to end.
5. Duration **115.0–125.0 s** (ffprobe `format.duration`); picture and sound streams the same length within 0.1 s.
6. English subtitles **burned into the picture**: every spoken line (narration and dialogue) on screen while it is spoken, readable (white with dark outline or shadow, lower third, never covering a face in close-up, ≤ 2 lines, ≤ ~42 characters per line). A sidecar `film.srt` with the same text in the editor's repository is welcome but does not replace the burn-in.

## The story — must
7. Every beat of the script (`direction/script.md`) is on screen and in the narration, in order: the freezing New Year's Eve street; the bare feet and lost slippers (carriages, the boy); no sales; the lit windows and roast goose; the corner and her fear of going home; five strikes — 1 stove, 2 roast goose that hops toward her, 3 Christmas tree whose lights become stars, a falling star and "Someone is dying", 4 the grandmother and "Grandmother! Take me with you!", 5 the whole bundle; the ascent with grandmother; the cold morning, her smile, the people and "She wanted to warm herself"; the closing line.
8. A first-time viewer follows it without knowing the tale, and is moved — judged by the test-audience screening and by the director's own viewing.

## Sound — must
9. Spoken English narration is the main voice, intelligible throughout; the three dialogue lines are present and in character.
10. Music and sound effects are present and support the beats (each match strike is heard).
11. Speech is in sync with its subtitles (± 0.25 s at start) and with any visible lip movement; effects in sync with their picture events (strikes, carriages) within 2 frames where visible.
12. Integrated loudness −16 LUFS ± 2 (EBU R128 / ITU-R BS.1770), true peak ≤ −1 dBTP, no clipping, no unintended silence or dropouts, no level jumps between shots.

## Picture — must
13. The girl, the grandmother, the corner, the street and the painted-storybook style stay consistent from shot to shot (same face, hair, dress, apron, bare feet; same wall; same palette logic). No shot breaks style (photoreal, 3D, anime) or identity.
14. No visible generation faults that a viewer would notice: melting or extra limbs and fingers, faces that morph, garbled text or signage, watermarks, frozen or repeated frames, flashes, black frames except the end title and intended fades.
15. No content beyond the tale's tone: the beating is never shown, the death is peaceful and not graphic.

## Should
- Cuts and transitions motivated (match flare and match-out as transitions); no jump cuts inside a shot.
- End title "The Little Match Girl / after Hans Christian Andersen".
- Running time close to 120 s.

## How the lock is decided
The director locks only a cut that: qc has checked against points 1–15 with a lock recommendation (or with faults the director has judged and accepted in the lock record); test-audience has watched and reported on; and the director has watched in full, picture and sound, start to end. The lock record (`lock/`) names the release, revisions, checks, screening notes and conclusion.
